#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353} :activity/id #uuid "0e333d7b-9586-5dcc-a2e9-29e8919f1fde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050442 :topic/id #uuid "49568fcd-874d-513a-8a2c-8089246092fb" :topic/math-academy-id 3674} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180792 :tutorial/content "Recall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis called a logistic growth equation. The positive constant $a > 0$ is the carrying capacity, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below. \n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the general solution to this equation is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\frac{a}{1+C e^{-ak t}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integration constant. \n\nNote the following properties of this general solution:\n\n- Since $a$ and $k$ are both positive, $e^{-akt}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, $y(t)\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ In other words, $y(t)$ approaches the carrying capacity as $t$ increases. This agrees with the behavior shown in our diagram.\n\n- Since $e^{-akt}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty,$ we have that $y(t)\\to 0$ as $t\\to-\\infty.$ This also agrees with our diagram.\n\nWe can use the general solution of the logistic growth equation to make predictions about a population governed by logistic growth. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ba42b62-b5a3-5c54-8a92-9ce67945fd36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3acea89b-a4db-5284-92df-1f9665ae536c" :step/math-academy-id 1924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179532 :question/id #uuid "77bb1db2-e2fb-5126-a116-fce07c309d0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539006}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"} :step/id #uuid "a534edd7-0a0d-55a8-a499-ecfc5a548c59" :step/math-academy-id 27476 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179533 :question/id #uuid "e53b71f8-4ea8-50e7-81c6-62fbd2ac54ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-7408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538993}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "b4f05448-8bec-515f-b01f-973c815d0ffd" :step/math-academy-id 27483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180682 :tutorial/content "The general solution to the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C>0$ is an integration constant. Let's now derive this formula.\n\nOur differential equation is separable, so we start by separating variables and then integrating with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =ky(a-y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =k \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ 1}{y(a-y)} = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{a-y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the integrand on the left-hand side and complete the integration, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{a}\\int (\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{a-y})\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y-a})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ -\\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =-ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|y-a|-\\operatorname{ln}|y| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|\\frac{y-a}{y}| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{-akt+{C}_{1}} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm C{e}^{-akt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=e^{C_1}$ is a positive constant. \n\nIn our modeling, we have assumed that $0 < y  < a.$ Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation must be negative since the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we discard the positive option, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-a}{y}  =  -C e^{-akt}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-a}{y} & =-C{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y-a & =-Cy{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y+y\\cdot C{e}^{-akt} & =a \\\\ y(1+C{e}^{-akt}) & =a \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba0b74de-5c49-5d2e-a9a6-207220dbb90e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4521 :tutorial/title "Deriving the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "8237a905-9644-5c44-a21f-8bcd1b0638a5" :step/math-academy-id 27816}] :activity/title "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179831}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539073} :activity/id #uuid "995e9bad-2373-581b-bf84-a4c6b91b00ca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050442 :topic/id #uuid "49568fcd-874d-513a-8a2c-8089246092fb" :topic/math-academy-id 3674} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea8eee30-c4b3-5768-b0a1-f0b46ef787ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538767} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea0a3a4-ab47-57c2-94ec-2bcd3b16dd3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa31a49a-8ce4-5ed7-acd6-a1443ddcc296" :question/math-academy-id "q-353252" :question/problem "The growth rate of the rabbit population $P(t)$ in a wildlife preserve is jointly proportional to the rabbit population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the preserve’s carrying capacity and the rabbit population.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.0025$ and the carrying capacity is $1200,$ find an expression for the rabbit population after $t$ years, given that there were $200$ rabbits in the preserve initially.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\" rowspan=\"2\">\n      $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1200$\n    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}}\n    </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Since the rabbit population’s growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the rabbit population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the rabbit population, the rabbit population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0025,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1200.$ Therefore, the rabbit population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0025\\,P(1200-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-(1200\\cdot 0.0025)t}}=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=200$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}} & =200 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1200}{200} \\\\ C & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04336f38-b31b-593f-9ab3-b5f823db5db4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7d2a3a-2e9b-513d-aefc-deb7330c3256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351658} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd4e14d-f873-5392-91f7-897cf31de18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351965} {:answer/id #uuid "c73b56a5-cac1-5b5a-8072-9761fae2a107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.5t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353293} {:answer/id #uuid "da7e22be-49d9-5065-8d9b-091acb94dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353464} {:answer/id #uuid "e700e7cb-c861-580f-9a2b-f525130e76a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce8c811-8797-5860-954a-946f44e6804b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c1c2a21-498a-5ae4-81c4-0f8dccba7406" :question/math-academy-id "q-83875" :question/problem "The growth rate of the mouse population $P(t)$ in a certain field is jointly proportional to the mouse population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the field's carrying capacity and the mouse population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,062\\,5$ and the carrying capacity is $8\\,000,$ then find an expression for the mouse population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ mice in the field initially." :question/worked-solution "Since the mouse population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the mouse population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the mouse population, the mouse population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,062\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8\\,000.$ Therefore, the mouse population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,062\\,5P(8\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(8\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,062\\,5)t}}=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{8\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "802436f1-98e0-504b-aa2c-e9887aff13d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ce7a4e-a22b-5aa7-8fe5-e882ab1347b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538369} {:answer/id #uuid "447be6ec-1715-5079-9b25-3bdc92bb6ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "73\\,172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538371} {:answer/id #uuid "7b129aba-1e60-5571-b939-cfee40621ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538515} {:answer/id #uuid "969e13f3-1d67-58f7-bd3f-d60dbd798efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,425" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538587} {:answer/id #uuid "df1451ad-15fb-555b-96d8-756c3d46621e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19\\,841" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538744} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a04f500-6386-50b3-90bc-dc19f72347e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59e76c83-715a-52bc-a7bd-8ad37145a622" :question/math-academy-id "q-83971" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$  infected with a certain virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus started spreading. If only one person was infected initially, how many people will have been infected after $5$ days?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(200\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(200\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2(0)}} & =1 \\\\ 1+C & =200\\,000 \\\\ C & =199\\,999\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=5$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2\\cdot 5}} \\\\  & ≈19\\,841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $5$ days, approximately $19\\,841$ people have been infected."} :step/id #uuid "b8859e7a-7cc5-5366-a8fa-22fef42311e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7e4caa-e1da-5f45-921e-9b9712388275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "149" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538269} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58b01f-da49-59b6-926d-d0d2cff7e680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "179" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538392} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3e4d59-1a5f-5d73-a41d-09dcaa86c1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538568} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f99c67-8f87-59fe-96cb-75f1084c69fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538766} {:answer/id #uuid "f46432d2-b7c1-5512-96c3-06133024f2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "116" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538784}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538392} :answer-field/id #uuid "eddb9464-ca92-530f-b8d3-62388de749ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80763a51-c6ab-5b5d-83cb-358601dd22aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120240" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ with food poisoning in an small town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the first reported cases. If there were $100$ cases initially, how many people with food poisoning there are after $4$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,03P(5\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =100 \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =50 \\\\ C & =49\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=4$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15(4)}} \\\\  & ≈179.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ weeks, there will be approximately $179$ people with food poisoning."} :step/id #uuid "d6c5eaaa-f548-59d3-bd7e-ee30f70ae2aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae9d1ff5-6c10-59e0-b65c-31cf4ae54efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "561" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538644} :answer-field/id #uuid "34bc565b-3149-51bd-8b80-246eddcdca66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c384ade7-8329-5b20-a7ce-55cc1c6684d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-353266" :question/problem "The growth rate of the number of households $P(t)$ participating in a neighborhood composting program is jointly proportional to the number of participating households at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the program’s carrying capacity and the number of participating households. The constant of proportionality is $0.0005$ and the carrying capacity is $800.$\n\nHow many households will be participating after $7$ years given that there were $100$ households participating initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ years, there will be approximately \n{{field-1}} households participating in the program." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of households participating grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of households participating at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of households participating, the number of households participating follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.0005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =8 \\\\ C & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4(7)}} \\\\  & ≈561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ years, there will be approximately $561$ households participating in the program."} :step/id #uuid "f3c777e9-fa60-59c6-a92c-a85032d8c4d5"}] :activity/title "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538225}]}
